  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "accelerated-batch-computational-pathology-935b",
  "summary": "Epidemic models on networks are moving beyond Markovian and static-topology assumptions to capture memory and coevolving structure.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is epidemic dynamics on networks where standard Markovian or static assumptions fail. One preprint derives outbreak-size distributions for non-Markovian SIR epidemics on networks by mapping them to an effective Markovian process. A second preprint uses an agent-based model of a fatal disease with waning immunity on an evolving scale-free network, reporting an epidemic transition and loss of power-law degree structure as disease and topology coevolve. The published AgentBasedModeling.jl paper is a heterogeneous contribution: a Julia tool for stochastic continuous-time agent-based simulation that links intracellular reaction networks to population dynamics, addressing multiscale population simulation rather than network epidemiology directly. What is new across the set is treating topology and memory as dynamic or non-exponential ingredients of transmission rather than fixed background assumptions.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-08-02"
}
